    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, we're having a major issue on the sublimation line here in Vietnam. The heat press is set to the standard 200C for 40 seconds, but the color transfer is really inconsistent. Some batches look bright, but others are looking super faded or even completely washed out, like the ink didn't even make it to the fabric. We checked the pressure and it seems steady. Do you know what might be causing this loss of pigment?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The issue could be related to the specifics of the fabric type, ink quality, or even the sublimation paper itself. Try using a consistent sublimation transfer paper and ensure that the ink with your vinyl is at the right weight-per-oz for sublimation. At 200C, check if 100% polyester fabrics are producing a good transfer, since polyester can be more temperature-sensitive for sublimation. You can also experiment with slight temperature adjustments or pressure settings and test smaller areas to dial in the perfect process. Use a color-corrected sublimation printer or pre-test the fabric beforehand to ensure compatibility. If it's still inconsistent, inspect the ink dispensing mechanism for any clogs or software for temperature/pressure alignment issues.",
