    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "We've already checked the fabric and the ink—everything is 100% polyester and from our usual supplier, so that shouldn't be the issue. The weirdest part is that it’s not a gradual fade; it’s like the ink just vanishes from certain sections of the print entirely. Like, the paper has the color, but once it's pressed, those parts are just blank. It feels like something is actively interfering with the transfer during those 40 seconds. Any idea why the pigment would just disappear like that?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It sounds like this could be a problem with the thermal transfer process itself. High heat combined with the wrong pressure settings can cause the ink to distort, leading to an incomplete transfer after 40 seconds. Since the pressure is steady, the disappearing ink might be due to uneven application or remnants on the sublimation paper escaping into the atmosphere. Try lowering the temperature slightly to 195C or even 190C to see if a cooler environment during printing gives you a full color transfer. Moreover, examine the might be an obstruction within the printhead or the finicky alignment for evenly distributing the heat across the print area. Ensure that every section of the printhead is working properly; sometimes individual nozzles can stick or clog momentarily, giving inconsistent results. Additionally, closely inspect the surroundings both inside and around the heat press for any drafts, air conditioning vents, or other direct airflow that could impact heat retention in certain zones. Another Crucial step would be to screen the sublimation ink tube with a flask to catch the pink pit mist or excess ink that's led to the transfer loss upon pressing. A combination of these adjustments should hopefully rectify the disappearing ink issue.",
      "source": "target",
